    {
      "id": "4907biy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.2 Physiographic Divisions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c5lfvxp",
      "content": "Chhattisgarh can be divided into four main physiographic regions:"
    },
    {
      "id": "n273b1f",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. Northern Hills (Chhattisgarh Plateau - North)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9af3rv2",
      "content": "The northern hills region includes the Korea, Sarguja, Jashpur, and Raigarh districts. The Mainpat plateau is located in this region, often called the 'Shimla of Chhattisgarh'. The highest peak of Chhattisgarh, Gaurlata (1,225 m), is located in the Surguja district."
    },
    {
      "id": "6s3l660",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. Chhattisgarh Plain (Maidan)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9nua3b1",
      "content": "The central plain, known as the Chhattisgarh Maidan, is the most fertile and densely populated region. It is drained by the Mahanadi river and its tributaries. The plain extends through Raipur, Durg, Rajnandgaon, Bilaspur, and adjacent districts. This region is the agricultural heartland and is known as the rice bowl of Chhattisgarh."
    },
    {
      "id": "s8hr6mk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. Dandakaranya (Southern Hilly Region)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a51xyoa",
      "content": "The Dandakaranya region encompasses Bastar, Dantewada, Bijapur, Sukma, Narayanpur, and Kondagaon districts. It is largely forested and home to several tribal communities. The Indravati river flows through this region. The Bailadila hills in Dantewada are famous for iron ore deposits."
    },
    {
      "id": "u9r7e8a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. Jashpur Plateau (Eastern Hills)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q5m2t9p",
      "content": "The Jashpur plateau lies to the northeast. The Pat Highlands (Mainpat, Jashpurnagar) are part of this region. The area receives heavy rainfall and is known for its dense forests."
    },
